<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>. ValidationRule.Description is
        capped at 255 characters; a comment is repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata
        API. It must sit INSIDE the root element. A comment placed ABOVE <ValidationRule>
        breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with a misleading "unable to find matching parent xml
        file" error.

        =====================================================================================
        ADDED 2026-08-20 (Tranche 2, Workstream A4). THE FIRST VALIDATION RULE ON THIS OBJECT.
        =====================================================================================
        Execution_Date__c is a RECORD OF FACT: the day the PSA was signed by both parties. A
        future date is therefore not a plan, it is a typo, and it is a load bearing one.
        OpportunityDocStatusController reads it as the contract date (falling back to
        PSA_Date__c only when it is null), and Opportunity_Record_Page surfaces it through
        Primary_Contract__r. A future execution date reports a deal as contracted on a day
        that has not happened.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 THIS RULE FIRES ON ACQUISITION PSAs AS WELL AS DISPOSITION PSAs, AND THAT IS
        INTENDED (requirements doc open item O-6).
        =====================================================================================
        Contract_Review__c is dual record type (Acquisition_PSA and Disposition_PSA). This
        rule carries no record type criterion because a future execution date is wrong on
        BOTH paths for the same reason: the field records something that has already happened.
        There is no reading of either workflow in which a PSA is executed next week.
        🔴 DO NOT ADD A RecordType.DeveloperName TEST "FOR SAFETY". It would also block Master
        record type rows, which TestDataFactory deliberately produces when a record type is
        absent from or unavailable in a scratch org, and the repo has the argument written out
        in Broker_Lookup_Is_Off_Market_Only's header.

        =====================================================================================
        ⚠ THIS RULE DELIBERATELY TOUCHES NEITHER Stage__c NOR Negotiation_Status__c. CHECKED,
        NOT ASSUMED, BECAUSE ONE OF THEM IS A TRAP.
        =====================================================================================
        Contract_Review__c.Stage__c is DERIVED: the before save flow Contract_Review_Stage_Sync
        recomputes it on EVERY save from Negotiation_Status__c. A rule keyed on Stage__c would
        be testing a value the platform is rewriting in the same transaction, and this repo has
        already measured a write to that field being silently discarded with a success toast.
        Negotiation_Status__c is the authored field and would have been the correct key IF this
        rule needed a status at all. It does not: "not in the future" is true at every status,
        so the simplest correct rule names no status.

        🔴 AND THE SAME FLOW IS WHY THIS RULE CANNOT FIGHT THE AUTOMATION. Its
        Is_Executed_Needs_Date branch stamps Execution_Date__c = $Flow.CurrentDate, i.e.
        TODAY, and only when the field is blank. Before save flow assignments run BEFORE
        validation rules in the save order, so the value this rule evaluates is today's date,
        and TODAY() is not greater than TODAY(). The stamp can never trip this rule.
        ⚠ IF THAT ASSIGNMENT IS EVER CHANGED TO AN OFFSET DATE, this rule breaks the flow.
        Re-read this paragraph before editing Set_Stage_Executed.

        =====================================================================================
        NO ISCHANGED / ISNEW GUARD. An INVARIANT, not a transition, so a pre existing bad row
        stays visible to the rule rather than being grandfathered. SELF CLEARING: the remedy,
        correcting the date, is itself a permitted save, so the rule cannot trap a record.
        ⚠ SYSTEM_MODE DML does not bypass validation rules, so a bad row would refuse machine
        writes too until corrected. Intended.
        ⚠ ONE EXCEPTION WORTH KNOWING, the same one Broker_Lookup_Is_Off_Market_Only records:
        a row locked by a pending approval cannot be edited by a non admin, so on such a row
        the recall has to come first.
        =====================================================================================
        MEASURED IMPACT AT CREATION: ZERO ROWS. Queried on usman-dpeg, 2026-08-20:
          SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM Contract_Review__c                              -> 9
          SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM Contract_Review__c WHERE Execution_Date__c &gt; TODAY -> 0
        Nine PSAs exist, none dated in the future, so this rule refuses nothing that exists.
        🔴 That measurement ages; re-run at deploy time. A non zero result freezes a live PSA
        until someone corrects its date, which is an escalation, not a deploy.
    -->
    <fullName>Execution_Date_Not_In_Future</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Execution Date records the day the PSA was actually signed, so it cannot be in the future. Fires on both record types by design: a future execution date is wrong on an acquisition PSA and a disposition PSA alike. Blank is untouched.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    NOT(ISBLANK(Execution_Date__c)),
    Execution_Date__c &gt; TODAY()
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorMessage>Execution Date cannot be in the future. It records the day the PSA was actually executed by both parties. Leave it blank until execution happens; it is stamped automatically when the status is set to Executed.</errorMessage>
    <errorDisplayField>Execution_Date__c</errorDisplayField>
</ValidationRule>
